# Contributing Workflow

Canonical owner for day-to-day git collaboration. Changelog format: `changelog.mdc`. Tagging / GitHub releases: `github-release.mdc`.

## Commits

- Use **conventional commits**: `feat`, `fix`, `chore`, `docs`, `refactor`, `test`, `perf`, `ci`.
- Subject: imperative, concise; body explains why when non-obvious.
- Reference issues when applicable (GitHub `#123` / GitLab `#123`).
- Keep commits focused — do not mix unrelated refactors with feature work.

## Branches

- One purpose per branch (single feature, fix, or chore).
- Prefer short names: `feat/<topic>`, `fix/<topic>`, `chore/<topic>`.
- Rebase or merge from the default branch before opening/updating a PR/MR when history is noisy.

## Pull / Merge Requests

- Small, reviewable diffs with a clear purpose: one PR moves one small chunk of change, never a whole plan.
- Description: what changed, why, how to test; link issues.
- Update the description when behavior changes during review.
- Do not include secrets, large generated artifacts, or unrelated lockfile churn unless the change requires it.

## Findings workflow (bugs / issues / improvements)

1. Create a GitHub issue per finding **before** fixing (`gh issue create`).
2. Fix on a focused branch.
3. Open a PR that closes the issue (`Closes #N`).
4. Merge the PR once checks pass (`gh pr merge`). The repo has no required checks, so `gh pr merge --auto` merges at once: watch the checks first (`gh pr checks <N> --watch`), then merge.
5. Update the issue checklist and result note before or right after the merge (see below).

Large findings: split the work into small PRs.

- Several PRs may reference one issue. Use `Part of #N` in each PR and `Closes #N` only in the last one; list the chunks as a checklist in the issue.
- Or split the finding into several issues, one per chunk, each closed by its own PR.

### Integration branch for multi-PR issues

When one issue is split into several PRs, do not merge the chunks into the working branch one by one.

1. Ask the user which branch is the **working branch** (`development`, `main` or `master`) unless the repo or the conversation already states it.
2. Create an integration branch from the working branch: `feat|fix|chore|ci/<topic>`, named after the issue.
3. Open every chunk PR against the integration branch with `Part of #N`. Watch the checks (`gh pr checks <N> --watch` until none is pending), then merge each chunk PR automatically. Tick the checklist item with each merge.
4. When every checklist item is ticked, open one final PR from the integration branch to the working branch with `Closes #N`. Ask the user who to assign it to (GitHub login or name), assign it with `gh pr create --assignee <login>`, and do **not** merge it: the assignee reviews and merges.
5. Delete the integration branch after the final PR merges.

Single-PR issues skip this and follow the findings workflow above.

- Update the issue checklist with every PR: tick the item the PR completes, note the result (for example measured timings) and list the next chunk. Do it before the merge when the PR's checks already pass, otherwise right after the merge. A merged PR never leaves its issue checklist stale.
- Keep an issue open until every checklist item is ticked. Put `Closes #N` only in the PR that completes the last item, and tick that item before merging; every other PR uses `Part of #N`. If a merge closes an issue that still has unticked items, reopen it (`gh issue reopen <N>`).
- Put `Closes #N`, `Fixes #N`, `Resolves #N` or `Part of #N` in the PR description: when the PR merges, `.github/workflows/pr-merged-issue-comment.yml` comments with the PR link on every issue it names. Ticking the checklist and noting results stay manual.

## Labels

Label every issue and PR (`gh issue create --label ...`, `gh pr create --label ...`); list them with `gh label list`.

- **Type** (one): `bug`, `enhancement`, `documentation`, `refactor`, `security`; add `breaking-change` when public behavior changes.
- **Area** (one or more): `area:<package>` for an adapter package (`area:kafka`, `area:redis`, ...), or `area:models`, `area:configs`, `area:helpers`, `area:testing`, `area:ci`.
- **Priority** (issues): `priority:high`, `priority:medium` or `priority:low`.
- A PR gets the labels of the issue it closes or is part of.

Exception: changelog-only PRs need no issue.

## Required checks before ask-to-merge

Prefer locally:

```bash
make format
make lint
make behave   # or targeted feature files
make security # when touching adapters, auth, crypto, or network code
```

Or `make check` / `make ci` for the full gate.

## Changelog & release handoff

- **Do not** update changelog every session, commit, or PR by default.
- Skip changelog for Cursor rules, `AGENTS.md`, internal chores, tests-only, and non-consumer docs unless the user says otherwise.
- Write or edit changelog **only** when the user explicitly asks for a changelog, version bump, or release.
- When asked: follow `changelog.mdc`. Do **not** create tags / GitHub releases until the draft is accepted — then `github-release.mdc`.
